@startuml claim-lifecycle
' How independent findings become one corroborated claim.
' Traced against src/afriend/merge.py (exact_merge) and
' src/afriend/ledger.py.

skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam defaultFontName "Helvetica"
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontSize 11
skinparam TitleFontSize 18
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

skinparam participant {
  BorderColor #546E7A
  BackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  FontSize 13
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #F9A825
  FontSize 12
}

title How corroboration survives deduplication

participant "friend A\nsecurity lens" as A #E8F5E9
participant "friend B\nops lens" as B #E8F5E9
participant "exact_merge" as M
database "claims.jsonl\nappend-only" as L #FFF8E1
participant "report.md" as R

A -> M : claim c-0001\n"the guard is missing"\norigin = [fake/security]
B -> M : claim c-0002\n"The Guard Is  Missing"\norigin = [fake/ops]

note over M
  Dedup key normalizes **only** whitespace
  runs and case, plus a strip on location.
  Nothing else — deliberate under-merge.
end note

M -> M : keys match →\nc-0002 aliases c-0001
M -> M : c-0001.origin grows to\n[fake/security, fake/ops]

M -> L : append claim c-0001
M -> L : append claim c-0002\n<b>(the duplicate too)</b>
M -> L : append alias\ncanonical=c-0001\nduplicate=c-0002

note over L
  The duplicate's own claim record is written,
  not just the alias. Otherwise alias.duplicate
  would dangle and a reader could not rebuild
  corroboration from the ledger alone.
end note

M -> R : one finding, ranked
R -> R : renders\n"corroborated by 2 friends"\nfake/security · fake/ops

note over R #E8F5E9
  Two independent models reaching the same
  conclusion is the strongest signal this
  tool produces — so it is never collapsed
  into a single unattributed claim.
end note

@enduml
